msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "In this tutorial you learned how to build a more complex scene, and how to write\nmovement code for a user-controlled character. You also learned about transforms, which are a very important concept in 3D - you're going to be using a lot in the future."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "Let's add one more movement to the player: jumping."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "Delete the {{< gd-icon Camera3D >}}`Camera3D` from the character scene and add a {{< gd-icon SpringArm3D >}}`SpringArm3D`. This node can act as a moving arm that holds the camera while detecting collisions. It will move the camera closer if there's an obstacle."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_move_02.gif)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "If you're curious how you would do this in first person, see the [Basic FPS Character](/godot_recipes/4.x/3d/basic_fps/index.html) recipe. You'll notice several similarities with the 3rd person script we wrote above."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "This will convert any mouse motion in the x direction into a rotation around\nthe **y** axis."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "This is all good, but we need to be able to rotate using the mouse. Add the following code to the character's script:"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "The code in `_physics_process()` is pretty straightforward: add gravity to\naccelerate in the positive **Y** direction (downward), call `get_input()` to\ncheck for input, and then use `move_and_slide()` to move in the direction\nof the velocity vector."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "In this picture, we've also added two sphere meshes for eyes to give a little more character. Feel free to add whatever details you like."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "Open the scene from the previous step with the \"Ground\" plane and the crates.\nDelete the crates and add an instance of the large block. We want to be able\nto place these blocks so that they line up. To do this, select \"Configure Snap\"\nfrom the \"Transform\" menu at the top of the Viewport and set _Translate Snap_\nto `0.5`. Then click on the \"Snap Mode\" button (or press the **`Y`** key). Now\nduplicate the block a few times and drag them to arrange."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "This is where _transforms_ come in. A transform is a mathematical _matrix_ that contains the object's translation, rotation, and scale information all in one. In Godot it's stored in the `Transform` data type. The position information is\ncalled the `transform.origin` and the orientation information is in the\n`transform.basis`."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "In the last part, we covered how to import 3D objects and how to arrange them in a scene. In this installment, we'll add more objects to the scene, including a user-controlled character."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "## Adding a Character"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "Add back a {{< gd-icon Camera3D >}}`Camera3D` as a child of the {{< gd-icon SpringArm3D >}}`SpringArm3D`, and try running the game again. You can experiment with rotating the spring arm (around its **x** axis to point down slightly, for example) until you find something you like."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "However, there's a problem. No matter which way we're facing, pressing **W**\nmoves us along the **Z** axis of the world. Our movement is using global coordinates, but we need to move in the _object's_ forward direction."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "Run the scene and confirm that moving the mouse rotates the character:"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "In its properties, set **Spring Length** to `5`, and set its **Position** to `(0, 1, 0)`, which is at the character's head. Note the yellow line indicating the **Spring Length**. The camera will move along this line - at its end whenever possible, but moving closer if an obstacle is there."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_scene_01.png)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "### What about first person?"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "We're going to continue using the Kenney Platformer Kit we downloaded in [Part 2](/godot_recipes/4.x/g101/3d/101_3d_02/). Select all the `block*.glb` files and in the **Import** tab set their **Root Type** to {{< gd-icon StaticBody3D >}}`StaticBody3D`. Uncheck the **Root Name** property and click **Reimport**. Select `blockLarge.glb` and make a new inherited scene. Use the **Create Single Convex Collision Sibling** option on the mesh using the menu as you did in the last tutorial. Now you can save the scene - I recommend making a separate folder for this, as soon you're going to have a bunch of scenes representing the differently shaped platform parts."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "### Improving the camera"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "The capsule is nice, but it's going to be hard to tell what direction it's\nfacing. Let's add another mesh, this time with a `CylinderMesh3D` shape.\nSet its **Top Radius** to `0.2`, its **Bottom Radius** to `0.001` and its **Height** to 0.5, then its **x** rotation to `-90` degrees. Now you have a nice cone shape. Arrange it so it's pointing out from the body along the *negative* **z** axis. (You can tell which way is negative because the gizmo arrows point in the positive direction)."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_move_03.gif)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_move_01.gif)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "If you like, go ahead and add scenes for some of the other platform blocks and\narrange them into a pleasing level. Be creative!"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/3d_character_01.png)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "You may have noticed that the if the character stands near an obstacle, the camera can \"clip\" inside the object, which doesn't look nice. While coding a good 3D camera can be a complex topic on its own, we can use a built-in Godot node to get a pretty good solution."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "In `get_input()` we check to see which key is pressed and then move in that\ndirection. Run the program and test:"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "Input Action | Key\n:------------|:---\nmove_forward | **W**\nmove_back | **S**\nstrafe_right | **D**\nstrafe_left | **A**\njump | **Space**"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_03.md
msgid "Remember how the 3D gizmo can be set to \"Local Space Mode\"? When in this mode,\nthe gizmo's **X/Y/Z** axes point along the _object's_ axes. This is the same as the `basis` of the transform. The basis contains three `Vector3` objects called `x`, `y`, and `z` that represent these directions. We can use this to ensure that pressing the **W** key will always move us in the object's forward direction."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_02.md
msgid "Also in the mesh properties is a **Material** property. Materials are how you\ndefine the appearance of an object. Select \"New StandardMaterial3D\" and then click it to open a large list of properties. To set the color of the mesh, we need the **Albedo/Color** property. Choose a color, such as brown or dark green."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_02.md
msgid "Run the scene again, and you'll be able to see your crates falling."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_02.md
msgid "## Building a 3D Scene"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_02.md
msgid "Once you've downloaded the kit, you'll find that the objects inside are provided\nin a variety of different formats. Godot is able to use several of these, but\nsince GLTF is available in this pack, it's preferred over the others. Drop the\n`GLTF format` folder into your Godot project's folder and rename it to \"platformer_kit\"."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_02.md
msgid "Run the scene to see what happens."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_02.md
msgid "There are multiple light nodes available in 3D, which you can use to create a variety of lighting effects. But we're going to start with {{< gd-icon DirectionalLight3D >}}`DirectionalLight3D`. However, instead of adding one manually, we're going to have Godot use the same one it's using in the editor window. At the top ove the viewport, there are two icons that control the *preview lighting* and *preview environment*. If you click the three dots next to them, you can see their settings."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_02.md
msgid "In the last part, we started a 3D project and looked at how to navigate and create 3D objects. In this part, you'll learn how to import existing 3D objects that you've made or downloaded and how to use more of Godot's 3D nodes."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_02.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/kenney_3d_platform_sample.png?width=100)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_02.md
msgid "Finally, we need to add a collision shape to the body. While we could do this by adding a {{< gd-icon CollionShape3D >}}`CollionShape3D`, as you would typically do in 2D, but there's a quicker way."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_02.md
msgid "In this tutorial you learned how to import 3D objects from outside sources, and\nhow to combine them into a simple scene. We also investigated lights and moving\ncameras."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_02.md
msgid "In the next part, we'll look at how to build a more complex scene and include\na player-controlled character."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_02.md
msgid "### Rotating Camera"
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_02.md
msgid "Let's make the camera a little more dynamic by having it slowly orbit around the\nscene. Select the root node and add a {{< gd-icon Node3D >}}`Node3D`, which will be located at `(0, 0, 0)` and name it\n\"CameraHub\". In the scene tree, drag the camera to make it a child of this new node. Now, if the {{< gd-icon Node3D >}}`CameraHub` rotates around the **y** axis, it will drag the camera along with it."
msgstr ""

#: g101/3d/101_3d_02.md
