msgid "{{% expand title=\"About angles\" %}}\n    When working with angles, rather than _degrees_, GDScript (like most programming languages) uses _radians_. In radians, a full rotation is equal to `2 * PI` (or `TAU`) - equivalent to `360` degrees.\n    {{% /expand %}}"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "That's a lot, so let's break it down:"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "Did you see the sprite moving to the right? Let's break down what's happening."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "## Using vectors"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "1. Since we want to rotate a random amount, we use `randf_range()` to get a random number between `0` and `TAU`."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "1. Finally, we multiply `velocity` by another random number to give it a random speed."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "What are we asking Godot to do every frame here? Take the sprite's `x` coordinate and add `100 * 1/60` to it."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "Now we want to make the sprite move around the screen. Godot runs at *60 fps* (frames per second) by default, and we'd like to the sprite to move a little bit each frame. Add this code to the script and run it to see what happens."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "In the previous step, we added a script to a {{< gd-icon Sprite2D >}}`Sprite2D` and used that script to set its position."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "It's often more convenient to declare variables at the top where they're easy to find. We'll also find it more convenient later, when we start making multiples of this sprite."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "We've taken this quantity and made it a variable. You **declare** a variable by using the keyword `var`, giving the variable a name, and then assigning a value with `=`."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "Run the code and you should see the sprite moving down and to the right."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "Let's make this a little easier to deal with by using **variables**:"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "Let's mix this up and choose a random direction for the sprite to move in."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "What if we want to move the sprite diagonally? That means we need to change the `x` and `y` coordinates at the same time. `position` is a **vector** - it contains both coordinates in one quantity. Vectors can be added, so let's change the code to this:"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_02.md
msgid "Just as the `_ready()` function was called when the node was first started (by pressing \"Play\"), the `_process()` function is called every frame. This means any code you write in this function will be executed every `1/60` of a second. This is what the `delta` represents - the length of the frame in seconds."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "Godot has a great built-in help/reference tool. Click on \"Classes\" at the top of the Script window and search for Node2D and you'll see a help page showing you all the properties and methods the class has available. Looking down a bit you can see `position` in the \"Member Variables\" section - that's the one we want. It also tells us the property is of the type \"Vector2\"."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "**Is GDScript Python?**"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "The first line of any GDScript file must be `extends <Class>`, where `<Class>` is some existing built-in or user-defined class. For example, if you're attaching a script to a {{< gd-icon CharacterBody2D >}}`CharacterBody2D` node, then your script would start with `extends CharacterBody2D`. This states that your script is taking all the functionality of the built-in `CharacterBody2D` object and *extending* it with additional functionality created by you."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "Now we see a function called `_ready()`. In GDScript you define a function with the keyword \"func\". The `_ready()` function is a special one that Godot looks for and runs whenever a node is added to the tree, for example when we hit \"Play\"."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "## Structure of a script"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "Go ahead and delete the comments and let's talk about this next piece."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "Since the script was added to a {{< gd-icon Sprite2D >}}`Sprite2D`, the first line is automatically set to `extends Sprite2D`.  Because this script extends the {{< gd-icon Sprite2D >}}`Sprite2D` class, it will be able to access and manipulate all the properties and methods that a {{< gd-icon Sprite2D >}}`Sprite2D` node provides."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "## Overview"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "Notice how the editor is making suggestions as you type. Godot uses vectors for lots of things, and we'll talk more about them later. For now, let's type Vector2, and the hint tells us to put two floats for `x` and `y`."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "Let's say that when the game starts, we want to make sure the sprite goes to a particular location. In the Inspector, we want to set the **Position** property. Notice that it's in the section called \"Node2D\" - that means this is a property that *any* {{< gd-icon Node2D >}}`Node2D` type node will have, not just {{< gd-icon Sprite2D >}}`Sprite2D`s."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "You'll often read comments to the effect that \"GDScript is based on Python\". That's somewhat misleading; GDScript uses a _syntax_ that's modeled on Python's, but it's a distinct language that's optimized for and integrated into the Godot engine. That said, if you already know some Python, you'll find GDScript looks very familiar."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "Writing scripts and attaching them to nodes and other objects is how you build behavior and game mechanics into your game. For example, a {{< gd-icon Sprite2D >}}`Sprite2D` node automatically displays an image, but to move it across the screen, you'll add a script that tells it how fast, in what direction, and so on."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "Now the script editor window opens up, and this is your new, empty sprite script. Godot has automatically included some lines of code, as well as some comments describing what they do."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "Next you need to decide where you want the script saved and what to call it. If you've named the node, the script will automatically be named to match it (so unless you've changed anything this script will likely be called \"sprite2d.gd\")."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "## Creating a script"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/gds_01_01.png)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "After that is where you're going to define all the variables you will use in the script, the \"member variables\". You define variables with the 'var' keyword - as you can see by the comment examples."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "{{% notice title=\"Properties and methods\" style=\"note\" %}}\n*Properties* and *methods* are two terms which specifically mean *variables* and *functions* that are defined in an object. Programmers tend to use the terms interchangeably.\n{{% /notice %}}"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/gds_01_03.png)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "{{% notice style=\"tip\" title=\"Learning tip\" %}}\nWhen first learning to code, beginners often ask \"How do you memorize all these commands?\" Just like any other skill, it's not a matter of memorization, it's about practice. As you use things more, the things you do frequently will \"stick\" and become automatic. Until then, it's a great idea to keep the reference docs handy. Use the search function whenever you see something you don't recognize. If you have multiple monitors, keep a copy of the [web docs](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/latest/) open on the side for quick reference.\n{{% /notice %}}"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "You can think of it as the coding version of using the Inspector - GDScript knows all about Godot nodes and how to access them, plus it allows you to change them dynamically."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "In the rest of the script, you can define any number of variables (aka \"class properties\") and functions (aka \"class methods\")."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "How do we set the property in code? One way to find the name of the property is by hovering over it in the Inspector."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "GDScript is Godot's built-in language for scripting and interacting with nodes. The [GDScript documentation](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/gdscript/gdscript_basics.html) on the Godot website is a great place to get an overview of the language, and I highly recommend taking the time to read through it."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "Let's go back to the script and use that property:"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "Now we have a script that says \"When this sprite starts, set its position to `(100, 150)`\". We can try this out by pressing the \"Play Scene\" button."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/gds_01_attach.png?width=250)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "Open the editor and add a {{< gd-icon Sprite2D >}}`Sprite2D` node to empty scene. Right-click on the new node, and choose \"Attach Script\". You can also click the button next to the search box."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/gds_01_02.png)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "{{% notice warning %}}\nMany tutorials (and Godot in general) assume that you have at least *some* programming experience already. If you've never coded before, you'll likely find learning Godot to be a challenge. Learning a game engine is a large task\non its own; learning to code at the same time means you're taking on a lot. If\nyou find yourself struggling with the code in this section, you may find that working through an introductory programming lesson (Python is a good option) will help you grasp the basics.\n{{% /notice %}}"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "Congratulations on making your first script in GDScript! Before moving on, make sure you understand everything we did in this step. In the next part, we'll add some more code to move the sprite around the screen."
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/gdscript_01.md
msgid "Let's make our first script. Remember, any node can have a script attached to it."
msgstr ""

