#: g101/start/_index.md
msgid "{{% notice style=\"info\" title=\"Updating to Godot 4.0\" %}}\nWe're working on a new version of Godot 101 for Godot 4.0. In the meantime, we recommend new learners stick with Godot 3.x, which has a lot more resources and learning materials available.\n{{% /notice %}}"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/_index.md
msgid "## <i class='fas fa-scroll'></i>&nbsp;GDScript"
msgstr ""

#: g101/gdscript/_index.md
msgid "GDScript is Godot's built-in scripting language. Its syntax is based on Python, so if you're familiar with that language, you'll feel right at home. In this chapter, we'll introduce the language and get you up to speed with how it works."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "* Platform Support"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "## Game Engines"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "* Development Environment"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "There are dozens of popular game engines to choose from today, such as Unity,\nUnreal, and GameMaker Studio, to name a few. It is important to remember that\nthe majority of popular engines are commercial products. They may or may not\nbe free to download, but the will require some kind of licensing or royalty\nagreement if you plan to release your game (and especially if your game makes\nmoney). You need to carefully read and understand what you're agreeing to and\nwhat you are and are not allowed to do with the engine."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "* Rendering (2D/3D)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "* Physics"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "In addition, there are a number of common needs every game has. For example,\nno matter what your game is, it's going to need to draw things on the screen.\nIf the code to do that has already been written, it makes more sense to reuse\nit that to create it all over again for every game. This is where game engines\ncome in."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "## Why use Godot?"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "In contrast to the above, Godot is completely free and open source, released\nunder the very permissive MIT license. This means there are no fees, hidden\ncosts, or royalties you need to pay. This is in addition to being a fully\nfeatured modern game engine."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "As a developer, the benefits are great. Because it's unencumbered by commercial\nlicensing, you have complete control over exactly how and where your game is\ndistributed. In addition, Godot's open source nature also means there is a much\ngreater level of transparency than you'll find with commercial engines. For\nexample, if you find a particular feature doesn't quite meet your needs, you're\nfree to modify the engine itself - no permission required."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "All of these tools are brought together in a single application, combining\neverything into one environment so you don't have to learn a new workflow for\nevery new project."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "Building an accurate and usable physics engine is an enormous task. Most games\nrequire some sort of collision detection and response, and many need simulated\nphysics (ie. friction, inertia, etc.), but few developers want to take on the\ntask of writing one."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "In today's market, you want to be able to release your game on multiple platforms, such as mobile, web, PC, and/or console. A game engine lets you build your game once and _export_ it to one or more platforms."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "Game development is complex and involves a wide variety of knowledge and skills.\nIn order to build a modern game, you need a lot of underlying technology before\nyou can make the actual game itself. Imagine if you had to build your own\ncomputer and write your own operating system before you could even start\nprogramming. Game development would be a lot like that if you truly had to\nstart from scratch and build _everything_ you needed."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "\"Rendering\" is the process of displaying your game on the player's screen. A\ngood rendering pipeline needs to work with modern GPU features, high resolution\ndisplays, and effects like lighting and perspective, while maintaining a high\nframe rate."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "A _game engine_ is a collection of tools and technologies designed to assist in\ndeveloping games. This allows you to focus more on building your game,\nand less on reinventing the wheel. Here are some of the features a good game\nengine will provide:"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_01.md
msgid "[![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/godot3_logo.png?width=250)](https://godotengine.org/)\n[Click here to download Godot](https://godotengine.org/)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "For example, you might have a player scene that contains all the nodes and scripts that make the player's character work. Then, you might create another scene that defines the game's map: the obstacles and items that the player must navigate through. A third scene might represent an enemy, of which you probably want to have many throughout the game."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "For example, a scene representing the player in your game might contain these nodes:"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "* GDScript is the dedicated built-in language, providing the tightest integration with the engine, and is the most straightforward to use. All of the examples on this site use GDScript. For the majority of projects, GDScript is the best choice of language. It is very tightly integrated with Godot's Application Programming Interface (API), and is designed for rapid development.\n* C# is best reserved for those users who already have a good understanding of Godot, and have a specific need. For those that do, Godot gives the flexibility to use a combination of GDScript and C# where you need them."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "For a more detailed introduction to GDScript, see the [Introduction to GDScript](/godot_recipes/4.x/g101/gdscript/index.html) on this site."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "If you are already familiar with one or more other programming languages, you should find GDScript very straightforward and easy to pick up. Godot is an object-oriented programming environment, so you should be comfortable with the concepts of classes and objects. The Godot documentation has an overview page that summarizes the syntax here: [GDScript Reference](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/gdscript/gdscript_basics.html)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "1. It's a great way to manage complexity. When you're working on one scene, you don't need to worry about the others. You can focus on all the functionality of your player character without getting distracted by other parts of the game.\n1. Since scenes are self-contained objects, you can use them as if they were nodes. Adding the player to the level is as easy as adding any of Godot's built-in nodes.\n1. Many game objects are not unique - you'll want to have lots of them in the game. Whether you are doing it in the Godot editor or via code, adding copies of the scene object is easy to do."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "## Creating Scenes"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "Notice that the tab where you added the {{< gd-icon Sprite2D >}}`Sprite2D` in the previous section is called **Scene**. In this tab is where you add the nodes that you wish to use to build your scene."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "Don't worry if you don't know what all those nodes are for just yet. The point here is that each one brings some type of functionality that you might want your player object to have."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "All of your game's scenes and nodes exist together in the **scene tree**. You can think of the scene tree as representing your entire game - everything that's currently running and active."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "## Scripting in Godot"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "Nodes in scenes are organized in a tree-like structure. Each node may have any number of *child* nodes, while each node only has one *parent* node."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/g101_scene_example.png)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "## The Scene Tree"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "As you create new objects during gameplay, you'll add them to the scene tree. As things are destroyed, you'll likewise remove them from the tree."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "Scenes in Godot are typically used to create and organize the various game objects in your project."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "Godot supports two official programming languages that you can use for scripting nodes: **GDScript** and **C#**."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "Organizing your game objects in this way brings multiple benefits:"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_04.md
msgid "{{% notice style=\"note\" title=\"Learning to Code\" %}}\nIf you're new to coding entirely, then you may find that you need a bit more support. We recommend the [Harvard CS50 open courseware](https://cs50.harvard.edu/x). It's a free course that will give you a great foundation for programming in general. Don't be intimidated by the time commitment - the effort you put in up front will save you countless hours of frustration later on.\n{{% /notice %}}"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "The list is long, and it would be frustrating to have to drill down every time to find the node you need. Instead, you can use the search function to find it using a small number of characters. We're looking for the {{< gd-icon Sprite2D >}}`Sprite2D` node, so I'll just type \"sp\" and we'll jump right to it. Click \"Create\" to add the node."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "A group of nodes arranged in a \"tree\" structure like this is called a _Scene_. In the next part, we'll look at how you can use scenes to organize your game's objects into independent parts that all work together. You'll see this in practice was you work through the examples in later lessons."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/g101_01_spritemove.gif?width=250)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "## Scenes"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "Over in the viewport, the sprite doesn't look like much. A sprite's purpose is to display an image, or texture. As you can see in the Inspector, the **Texture** property is currently empty. Fortunately, every new Godot project comes with an image we can use: the Godot icon. Drag the icon from the Filesystem dock and drop it in the texture property."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "Notice that the **Position** of the parent is changing as you move it around. Now check the child: it's still `(50, 50)`. That's because its \"Transform\" properties are _relative_ to its parent."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "In the Inspector, click to expand the \"Transform\" section, and type `(50, 50)` in the **Position** property."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "One important property of nodes is that they can be arranged in a parent-child hierarchy. Make sure you have the {{< gd-icon Sprite2D >}}`Sprite2D` selected and press the add button again. Add another {{< gd-icon Sprite2D >}}`Sprite2D` and also drag the icon into its texture."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/g4_101_11.png)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "You can also click and drag the sprite around in the viewport, and you'll see the **Position** values changing as you move."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "Nodes are _objects_, in the programming sense. They encapsulate data and behavior, and they can inherit properties from other nodes. Rather than use one of the default suggestions, let's click the \"Add/Create a New Node\" button in the scene dock."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/g4_101_06.png?width=500)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "Here you'll see the whole hierarchy of node types available in the engine. For example, the nodes with the bluish icons all fall under the \"Node2D\" category, meaning they will all have the properties of a Node2D. More about that in a moment."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/g4_101_09.png)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "## Working with Nodes"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/g4_101_07.png)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "Now we have this {{< gd-icon Sprite2D >}}`Sprite2D` node in our **Scene** dock. Make sure it's selected, and then look at the Inspector dock on the right side. Over here, you'll see all the properties of whatever node you have selected. Notice that the properties are organized by where they come from. The {{< gd-icon Sprite2D >}}`Sprite2D` node inherits from {{< gd-icon Node2D >}}`Node2D`, which inherits from {{< gd-icon CanvasItem >}}`CanvasItem`, which inherits from the plain old {{< gd-icon Node >}}`Node`."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "Nodes are the basic building blocks for creating games in Godot. A node is an object that can represent some kind of specialized game function. A given type of node might display graphics, play an animation, or represent a 3D model of an object. The node also contains a collection of properties, allowing you to customize its behavior. Which nodes you add to your project will depend on what functionality you need. It's a modular system designed to give you flexibility in building your game objects."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "Grouping nodes together like this is a powerful tool, enabling you to construct complex objects out of node \"building blocks\". For example, a \"Player\" node in your game might have many child nodes attached to it: a {{< gd-icon Sprite2D >}}`Sprite2D` for display, an {{< gd-icon AnimationPlayer >}}`AnimationPlayer` to animate it, a {{< gd-icon Camera2D >}}`Camera2D` to follow it around, and so on."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/g4_101_08.png?width=500)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "This new sprite is a _child_ of the first. This means that it's \"attached\" to its parent. If the parent sprite moves, so will the child. Click on the child sprite and set its **Position** to `(50, 50)`. Now click and drag the parent sprite to move it around the screen."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_03.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/g4_101_10.png)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_02.md
msgid "{{% notice note %}}\nEvery Godot project is contained in its own folder. This has many benefits, including making it easy to move, share, and backup\nprojects. It also means that all the project's files (images, sounds, etc.) _must_ be in the project folder.\n{{% /notice %}}"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_02.md
msgid "We'll come back to the project settings window later. Let's close it for now and we're ready to move on to the next step: working with nodes."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_02.md
msgid "![alt](/godot_recipes/4.x/img/g4_101_03.png?width=800)"
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_02.md
msgid "In here, you can set your game's title, choose which scene is the \"main scene\" (more about that in a bit), and change the icon."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_02.md
msgid "So let's click on **Project** in the menu and select **Project Settings**."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_02.md
msgid "Let's name this new project \"getting_started\". Type this name, click **Create Folder**, and then click **Create & Edit**."
msgstr ""

#: g101/start/101_02.md
msgid "* **Viewport:** This is where you'll see the parts of your game as you're working on them.\n* **Workspaces:** At the center-top, you can switch between working in the 2D, 3D, or Script workspaces. You start in 3D.\n* **Playtest Buttons:** These buttons let you launch and control your game when testing.\n* **Docks/Tabs:** On both sides are a number of docks where you can view game items and set their properties.\n* **Bottom Panel:** Here, you'll see context-specific information for various tools. The most important one to note first is the **Output** panel, where you'll see any error or informational messages when your game is running."
